//
// Expected: c0 at one session and at two.
//
// Alice seals two messages under one key and one nonce, but only the second
// ciphertext ever leaves her. The first is declared `forgeable`, and the
// attacker holds its plaintext, nonce and associated data, so it can mint a
// term equal to e1. That minted term repeats no keystream: the attacker made
// it itself, as in cap_forgeable_is_not_a_reuse_pair.vp, and Alice's own e1 is
// never disclosed in any form. So it is no reuse partner for e2, and m2 stays
// confidential. The engine once asked only whether a principal had built a
// mintable member, not whether the attacker held that principal's copy, and
// read m2 out of the pair. A reuse member now passes the same test as a
// decomposed term: built, and disclosed or genuine. The nonce is generated,
// so the second session pairs nothing either.
// cap_forgeable_sent_reuse_member.vp is the counterweight.
attacker[passive]
principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public ad
	generates n, m1, m2
	e1 = AEAD_ENC[forgeable](k, n, m1, ad)
	e2 = AEAD_ENC(k, n, m2, ad)
	leaks m1
]
Alice -> Bob: n, e2
principal Bob[
	_ = HASH(e2)
]
queries[
	confidentiality? m2
]
